Part 1 – Theory & Syntax
Lesson 1: WHERE Clause
Easy
Theory
WHERE clause ka use table se specific conditions ke according records retrieve karne ke liye hota hai.

Jab tumhe complete table nahi, balki sirf particular records chahiye hote hain, tab WHERE use karte hain.

Real-world analogy: Restaurant mein waiter ko bolna: "Mujhe sirf ₹200 se kam wali dishes dikhana."
Syntax
SELECT column_name
FROM table_name
WHERE condition;


Example 1: Specific city ke students
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE city = 'Delhi';


Example 2: Specific course ke students
SELECT full_name, course
FROM students
WHERE course = 'Full Stack Development';


Samjho execution:
students table

10 records


WHERE city = 'Delhi'

Condition checking


Matching records
Filtered result



Lesson 2: Comparison Operators
Theory
Comparison operators ka use do values compare karne ke liye hota hai. WHERE ke saath inka use karke hum numeric aur text conditions check kar sakte hain.

Analogy: Calculator mein check karna ki 500, 300 se bada hai ya nahi.
Operators
Operator	Meaning
=	Equal to
!=	Not equal to
<>	Not equal to
>	Greater than
<	Less than
>=	Greater than or equal
<=	Less than or equal

Examples
Example 1: Age greater than 22
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE age > 22;


Example 2: Fees less than or equal to 40000
SELECT full_name, fees
FROM students
WHERE fees <= 40000;


Example 3: Delhi ko chhodkar baaki cities
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE city != 'Delhi';


Important: NULL comparison
NULL ko normal = operator se compare nahi karte.
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE fees IS NULL;


Aur:
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE fees IS NOT NULL;


Is concept ko abhi basic level par samjho; NULL handling ko baad mein detail mein cover karenge.

Lesson 3: AND Operator
Theory
AND operator tab use hota hai jab multiple conditions ek saath satisfy honi chahiye.

Analogy: Job requirement: Candidate ki age 21+ bhi honi chahiye AND experience 2+ years bhi.
Syntax
SELECT *
FROM table_name
WHERE condition1 AND condition2;


Example
Students jinki age 22 se zyada hai aur city Delhi hai:
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE age > 22
AND city = 'Delhi';


Important: AND mein dono conditions TRUE honi chahiye.

Lesson 4: OR Operator
Theory
OR operator ka use tab hota hai jab multiple conditions mein se koi ek bhi TRUE ho toh record return ho sakta hai.

Analogy: Restaurant mein tum Pizza OR Burger order kar sakte ho.
Syntax
SELECT *
FROM table_name
WHERE condition1 OR condition2;


Example
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE city = 'Delhi'
OR city = 'Noida';


Yahan Delhi ya Noida mein se kisi bhi city ke students return honge.
AND vs OR
AND	OR
All conditions TRUE	At least one condition TRUE
More restrictive	Less restrictive


Lesson 5: NOT Operator
Theory
NOT operator kisi condition ka opposite result deta hai.

Analogy: "Mujhe Delhi ke students nahi chahiye."
Syntax
SELECT *
FROM table_name
WHERE NOT condition;


Example:
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE NOT city = 'Delhi';


Equivalent:
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE city != 'Delhi';



Lesson 6: IN Operator
Theory
IN operator multiple possible values ko check karne ke liye use hota hai. Ye multiple OR conditions ka cleaner alternative hai.

Analogy: Shopping list mein 3 acceptable brands select karna.
Syntax
SELECT *
FROM table_name
WHERE column_name IN (value1, value2, value3);


Example:
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE city IN ('Delhi', 'Noida', 'Mumbai');


Equivalent:
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE city = 'Delhi'
OR city = 'Noida'
OR city = 'Mumbai';


NOT IN:
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE city NOT IN ('Delhi', 'Noida');



Lesson 7: BETWEEN Operator
Theory
BETWEEN ka use kisi value ko specified range ke andar check karne ke liye hota hai.

Analogy: Exam mein 60 se 80 marks ke beech score karne wale students select karna.
Syntax
SELECT *
FROM table_name
WHERE column_name BETWEEN value1 AND value2;


Example:
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE age BETWEEN 20 AND 25;


Important
 BETWEEN inclusive hota hai. Matlab 20 aur 25 dono include honge.

NOT BETWEEN:
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE age NOT BETWEEN 20 AND 25;



Lesson 8: LIKE Operator
Theory
LIKE ka use text ke andar pattern matching ke liye hota hai. Jab exact value nahi pata hoti, lekin text ka kuch part pata hota hai, tab ye useful hai.

Analogy: Contact list mein aise log search karna jinka naam 'Rah' se start hota hai.
Wildcards
Wildcard	Meaning
%	Zero or more characters
_	Exactly one character

Examples
1. Name starts with A
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE full_name LIKE 'A%';


2. Name ends with a
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE full_name LIKE '%a';


3. Name contains 'ha'
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE full_name LIKE '%ha%';


4. Exactly one character wildcard
SELECT *
FROM students
WHERE full_name LIKE 'A_an';



Lesson 9: ORDER BY
Theory
ORDER BY ka use retrieved records ko ascending ya descending order mein arrange karne ke liye hota hai.

Analogy: School mein students ko marks ke highest-to-lowest order mein arrange karna.
Syntax
SELECT *
FROM table_name
ORDER BY column_name ASC;


ASC – Ascending
SELECT *
FROM students
ORDER BY age ASC;


Smallest to largest.
DESC – Descending
SELECT *
FROM students
ORDER BY fees DESC;


Largest to smallest.
Multiple columns sorting
SELECT *
FROM students
ORDER BY city ASC, age DESC;


Pehle city ascending, phir same city ke records age descending.

Lesson 10: LIMIT
Theory
LIMIT ka use query se return hone wale records ki maximum quantity control karne ke liye hota hai.

Analogy: YouTube par sirf top 5 search results dekhna.
Syntax
SELECT *
FROM table_name
LIMIT number;


Example:
SELECT *
FROM students
LIMIT 5;


LIMIT with OFFSET
SELECT *
FROM students
LIMIT 5 OFFSET 5;


Iska matlab: Pehle 5 records skip karo aur next 5 records return karo.
